---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Pricing';
const description =
  'Published rates. Builds from $2,500 to $8,000 depending on size; optimisation at $750, $2,400 or $2,900 per month. Market context and who each is wrong for.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How much does AEO cost in 2026?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Agency retainers for answer engine optimisation typically run $2,000 to $15,000 per month in 2026. Growth-stage engagements cluster at $3,000-$8,000 and enterprise scopes start near $15,000. Below roughly $1,500 per month you are generally buying rebranded SEO.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why publish prices when most agencies do not?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Hidden pricing exists so the number can be adjusted to what a buyer looks like they can pay. Publishing one rate for everyone removes that, filters out mismatched enquiries before they reach the inbox, and is difficult to fake.',
      },
    },
  ],
};

const rows = [
  ['Snippet Tester essential build', '$2,500 - $4,000', 'Up to 5 pages, static, scored before launch'],
  ['Snippet Tester standard build', '$4,000 - $8,000', '6-12 pages plus blog'],
  ['Typical freelance small business site', '$2,000 - $8,000', 'US market, 2026'],
  ['Typical 5-page brochure site, freelancer', '$3,000 - $6,000', '4-6 weeks'],
  ['Boutique agency, same brief', '$8,000 - $15,000', 'Template-based'],
  ['Custom build, small to mid-sized business', '$5,000 - $20,000', 'Bespoke design or functionality'],
  ['Snippet Tester audit', '$750 one-off', 'Diagnosis only, whole site, 5 days'],
  ['Snippet Tester sprint', '$2,400 one-off', 'Diagnosis plus implementation, 3-4 weeks'],
  ['Snippet Tester retainer', '$2,900 / month', 'Rolling, no minimum term'],
  ['Typical growth-stage agency', '$3,000 - $8,000 / month', 'Focused engagement, market rate'],
  ['Typical mid-market agency', '$2,000 - $10,000 / month', 'Content, technical, multi-engine monitoring'],
  ['Typical enterprise scope', '$15,000+ / month', 'Heavy off-site work'],
  ['Anything under $1,500 / month', 'Below market', 'Generally rebranded SEO'],
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[schema]}>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pricing</p>
      <h1>The rates are on the site. That is unusual, and it is deliberate.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In this category almost nobody publishes a number. The reason is that the number gets adjusted
        to what the buyer looks like they can afford. One published rate removes that possibility, so
        here it is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="prose">Website builds</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Builds are quoted, not sold off a shelf — the number depends on page count and how much of the
        copy you bring. The bands below are where projects land, and you get a fixed figure in writing
        before anything starts. Every build includes the 90+ checker score in the contract.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Essential build</p>
          <span class="price">$2,500<small>-4,000</small></span>
          <p class="small">Up to 5 pages · 3-4 weeks</p>
          <p>Home, services, about, contact and one more. Static, schema, scored before launch. You supply the facts, we shape the copy.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> you need a blog you update weekly.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact?plan=build-essential">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card--flag">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Standard build</p>
          <span class="price">$4,000<small>-8,000</small></span>
          <p class="small">6-12 pages plus blog · 5-7 weeks</p>
          <p>Everything above, plus a publishing setup, service or location pages, and copy structured page by page to answer specific questions.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> you need e-commerce or user accounts.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--accent" href="/contact?plan=build-standard">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Rebuild after an audit</p>
          <span class="price">Build price<small> less $750</small></span>
          <p class="small">Audit fee credited in full</p>
          <p>If the audit concludes your site cannot be optimised into shape, the $750 comes off the build. You never pay twice for the same diagnosis.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> the audit says fixing is cheaper — which it usually does.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact?plan=rebuild">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="prose" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">Optimising a site you already have</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Fixed prices, because diagnosis and repair are predictable in a way that a build is not.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Audit</p>
          <span class="price">$750</span>
          <p class="small">One-off · 5 working days</p>
          <p>Full structural diagnosis of every template, your measured AI Overview exposure, and a prioritised fix list.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> you already know what is broken and just need it done.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact?plan=audit">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card--flag">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Sprint · most people want this</p>
          <span class="price">$2,400</span>
          <p class="small">One-off · 3-4 weeks</p>
          <p>The audit plus the changes made: schema shipped, top 10 pages restructured, technical fixes live, before-and-after measured.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> you publish weekly and need someone keeping pace.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--accent" href="/contact?plan=sprint">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">Retainer</p>
          <span class="price">$2,900<small>/mo</small></span>
          <p class="small">Rolling · cancel any month</p>
          <p>Four to six pages a month, citation monitoring across four engines, schema maintained, reporting that leads with failures.</p>
          <p class="small"><strong>Wrong for you if</strong> your site is stable and small — buy the sprint once instead.</p>
          <p><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact?plan=retainer">Enquire</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="prose" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">How that compares to the market</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Published survey data for 2026, so you can see where these rates sit rather than taking my
        word for it.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Rate</th><th scope="col">Scope</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.map(([a, b, c]) => (
              <tr><td>{a}</td><td class="num">{b}</td><td class="small">{c}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note">
        Build market figures:
        <a href="https://www.jim.com/blog/small-business-website-cost" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">small business website cost survey 2026</a> and
        <a href="https://projectcostestimator.com/freelance-website-cost" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">freelance developer rate data</a>
        (hourly $75-$150 for experienced designers).
        Optimisation figures:
        <a href="https://humanswith.ai/blog/what-aeo-and-geo-actually-cost-in-2026/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">HumansWith.ai</a>
        and <a href="https://outreachbloom.com/aeo-geo-pricing-guide/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Outreach Bloom</a> AEO/GEO pricing surveys, 2026.
      </p>

      <div class="callout" style="max-width: var(--measure);">
        <p>
          <strong>Being cheaper is not the pitch.</strong> These rates sit at the low end of the market
          because I am one person with no overhead, not because the work is thinner. If you want a team,
          account management and a Slack channel with five people in it, hire an agency — that is a real
          thing to want and I cannot provide it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="prose" style="margin-top: 3rem;">Questions people ask before paying</h2>
      <div class="stack prose" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Do you guarantee results?</h3>
          <p>
            No, and neither can anyone else. Rankings and AI citations are decided by systems no vendor
            controls. What I guarantee is the deliverables list and that the measurement baseline is
            yours to keep.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What if the audit says my site is fine?</h3>
          <p>
            Then the audit says your site is fine, you have a document proving it, and I do not try to
            sell you a sprint. This happens and it is a good outcome for both of us.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What happens if a build misses the 90 score?</h3>
          <p>
            It does not launch until it clears. The threshold is in the contract, the tool is public,
            and you can verify it yourself the day after handover. That is the whole reason the
            checker is free rather than gated.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Why is the retainer rolling rather than a 6-month contract?</h3>
          <p>
            Because a minimum term protects the supplier, not you. If the work is producing something,
            you will stay.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
